• What the visitor does: scroll, click, tap, or keyboard input, stated separately when behaviors differ.
• Exactly what changes visually: viewpoint, apparent movement, focal point, subject position, scale, foreground/background layers, masks, openings, reveals, and text appearance or disappearance as applicable.
• How the current view connects to the next: what remains aligned, what leaves the frame, what becomes visible, and which additional images or layers make that possible.

• What counts as completing the action and arriving at the next state. Reveal destination content only after the promised visual action completes.
• What the next scene shows, how visitors continue or choose a branch, how they return, and how they reach the primary business action.

• What happens when input stops, reverses, or interrupts the transition, plus mobile, keyboard, skip, and reduced-motion behavior.
For every approved scroll-driven sequence (normally desktop only), give a finite 0-100% timeline with project-specific milestones. Explain total scroll travel in screen heights and provide a pixel example for a stated viewport height. Explain approximately how much progress one screen-height of scrolling produces, and any deliberate differences in rate across the sequence. State where pinning starts and ends, when ordinary content scrolling resumes, whether stopping holds the image, and how reverse scrolling behaves. Do not claim a universal number of mouse- & wheel ticks or seconds; wheel settings, gestures, and visitor speed vary. Label proposed timing and scroll-distance values as proposals until tested. When I request a scroll-driven visual action, design scrolling to visibly perform that action on the approved devices. Present the required compact mobile alternative separately; the mobile-first defaults apply unless I explicitly approve long scroll-driven motion on mobile. Do not substitute passive section scrolling, a decorative crop, a click-only transition, or an unexplained image swap. If the action cannot be achieved convincingly with available assets or capabilities, explain the specific limitation and propose a material change for agreement before building it. Do not pretend that a zoom or blend automatically produces a complete visual journey. Include an
interaction map, practical asset list, and implementation plan. Identify which key views already exist and which intermediate views, cutouts, masks, or mobile crops must still be created. Clearly distinguish illustrative storyboard frames from continuity-ready production assets, and proposed behavior from implemented or tested behavior.

